  Context: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is a single static page, no framework, no build step, no tests, and [38;5;153m<main>[39m is empty — there are no
  task items on the page yet.

  [1mThe fork:[0m you said "component," and in a plain HTML page that can mean two different things, which differ in whether
  this change introduces JavaScript at all.

  - [1mStatic markup only[0m — a [38;5;153m<label><input type="checkbox"> …</label>[39m written directly into [38;5;153m<main>[39m. The browser handles
    on/off for free; zero JS, nothing to maintain. Costs: it's one hardcoded checkbox, so adding items later means
    hand-writing more markup.
  - [1mA JS factory[0m — a small [38;5;153mcreateCheckbox(label)[39m function plus a [38;5;153m<script>[39m that appends one. Buys reuse when you start
    rendering a real task list. Costs: introduces a script and a state-ownership question (does the DOM hold the checked
    state, or a JS array?) that you'd otherwise defer.

  Both are cheap to change later — going from static markup to a factory is a few minutes of work — so I'd lean static
  markup and let a real task list drive the JS when you actually have one.
